 "findings": [
  "The candidate is 2.06x faster than the promoted dp4a on the gate/up leg (353.83 vs 171.59 GB/s at production geometry), drift max 3.0517578125e-05 (one bf16 ulp) and p999 0.0 in-block.",
  "Against DE-1's own e2e traffic model (888.83 MB/step, dp4a part 5.422 ms/step) that recomputes to ~2.79 ms/step saved, about 5.9% of the 47.359 ms/step decode kernel total.",
  "But the candidate is a measured numerical regression on the max tail: 4.34x worse than the incumbent on heldout_mixed_review and 3.55x worse on mixed_ja_en_review, with an extra top-1 flip.",
  "The entire tail lives in 4 mixed_ja_en cases; the other 14 cases are all below 0.0094 for the candidate and the two arms track each other (ratios 0.16-2.52).",
  "The 18-case admission suite is ALSO failed by the production incumbent (3 cases exceed the 0.05 ceiling: 0.07849, 0.06017, 0.05364), so it is a stress suite, not an admission basis, and its bars do not discriminate between the two arms.",
  "DE-1's 184.66 GB/s comparator is NOT a roof: the warp256 exceeds it by 1.92x, so the 2026-09-29 conclusion that 93.7% of headroom was consumed and only 0.591 ms/step remained understated the remaining opportunity roughly five-fold.",
  "The 996-row packet cited by the flag's docstring and by docs/EXECUTION-PROFILES.md 6.1 used the canonical 12 cases, which do not include the heldout_mixed_review stress case; its qualification was therefore narrower than the docstring's wording implies."
 ],
 "promotion_decision": "do_not_promote",
 "next": "The 2.06x speed win is real and worth recovering, but it needs a route that does not inflate the mixed_ja_en max tail. The candidate's arithmetic is one bf16 ulp of the incumbent in-block, so the tail is a route-flip sensitivity, not a per-element error: the two mixed_ja_en cases that regress are exactly the ones where the MoE router's expert choice moves. Any follow-up should localize which layer and which expert transition flips, and test whether a narrower layer scope (like the 43-of-48 dp4a layer list) avoids the sensitive layers."
}
